        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 776,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the standard deviation of an array, ignoring any nan (\"not a number\") in it.\n\nnp.std gives nan as soon as a single value is nan - a dropped sensor reading, an empty slot, a division by zero upstream - so one gap spoils the whole answer. np.nanstd leaves the gaps out and works with the values that are there. Only a row or column made entirely of nan gives nan.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.nanstd <axis: int>\nDefault: axis None (the whole array).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is measured and sent.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nNone measures over every element. A number measures along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one answer per column, 1 one per row.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nnanstd:\nThe standard deviation: a single value, or one per column or row.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n3 nan 1 7 has a gap. np.std gives nan; np.nanstd gives 2.49, the standard deviation of 3 1 7. The round node only tidies the number. A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.std is the version that does not skip gaps. np.nanvar, np.nanmean, np.nanmedian and np.nansum skip them the same way. np.isnan finds the gaps;\nnp.nan_to_num fills them.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the standard deviation of an array, ignoring any nan (\"not a number\") in it.\n\nnp.std gives nan as soon as a single value is nan - a dropped sensor reading, an empty slot, a division by zero upstream - so one gap spoils the whole answer. np.nanstd leaves the gaps out and works with the values that are there. Only a row or column made entirely of nan gives nan.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.nanstd <axis: int>\nDefault: axis None (the whole array).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is measured and sent.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nNone measures over every element. A number measures along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one answer per column, 1 one per row.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nnanstd:\nThe standard deviation: a single value, or one per column or row.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n3 nan 1 7 has a gap. np.std gives nan; np.nanstd gives 2.49, the standard deviation of 3 1 7. The round node only tidies the number. A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.std is the version that does not skip gaps. np.nanvar, np.nanmean, np.nanmedian and np.nansum skip them the same way. np.isnan finds the gaps;\nnp.nan_to_num fills them."
        },
